.TH SPARKLE-CLEAN 1 "September 2026" "sparkle-clean 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sparkle-clean \- free up disk space safely
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sparkle-clean
.br
.B sparkle-clean scan
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B sparkle-clean clean
.RB [ \-y ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B sparkle-clean categories
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B sparkle-clean history
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sparkle-clean
finds files that are safe to remove on Ubuntu and shows how much space each
kind takes before anything is deleted.
Run without arguments to open the desktop app.
The commands below provide the same functionality in a terminal.
.PP
Scanning never changes anything.
Cleaning always shows what will be removed and asks for confirmation first.
System files are cleaned by a helper that runs as root through
.BR pkexec (1),
so you are asked for an administrator password once per cleanup.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B scan
Measure every category and print what can be cleaned.
.TP
.B clean
Scan, list what will be deleted, ask you to type
.B yes
and clean.
Exits with status 1 if any category could not be cleaned.
.TP
.B categories
List the cleanup categories, their ids and whether they are selected.
.TP
.B history
List past cleanups and the space they freed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-only " IDS"
Only use these categories (comma-separated ids).
.TP
.BI \-\-include " IDS"
Add categories that are off by default, such as
.BR browsers .
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " IDS"
Skip these categories.
.TP
.BI \-\-tmp\-age\-days " DAYS"
Only remove temporary files that have not been read, written or changed for
this many days (1\(en365).
.TP
.BI \-\-journal\-days " DAYS"
Keep this many days of system journal history (1\(en365).
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-age\-days " DAYS"
Only remove rotated logs older than this many days; 0 removes them regardless
of age (0\(en365).
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask for confirmation
.RB ( clean
only).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be cleaned and stop
.RB ( clean
only).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print the output of the system commands that run during cleanup.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print machine-readable JSON.
.B clean \-\-json
also needs
.B \-\-yes
or
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Never use colors. Colors are also off when output is not a terminal or
.B NO_COLOR
is set.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
Without options, the selection and limits saved in the desktop app are used.
.SH CATEGORIES
.SS System
.TP
.B apt_cache
Downloaded package files in /var/cache/apt/archives
.RB ( "apt-get clean" ).
.TP
.B orphans
Automatically installed packages nothing depends on anymore
.RB ( "apt-get autoremove \-\-purge" ).
.TP
.B snaps
Disabled snap revisions kept after updates.
.TP
.B journal
Archived system journal files older than the retention period
.RB ( "journalctl \-\-vacuum\-time" ).
.TP
.B old_logs
Rotated logs in /var/log matching *.gz, *.[0\-9] and *.old.
.TP
.B tmp
Files in /tmp and /var/tmp unused for the configured number of days.
Files that a program has open, X11 lock files, and the private folders of
systemd services and snaps are always kept.
.TP
.B crash
Crash reports in /var/crash.
.TP
.B flatpak
Unused Flatpak runtimes and extensions
.RB ( "flatpak uninstall \-\-unused" ).
.SS Personal
.TP
.B trash
Everything in your Trash.
.TP
.B thumbnails
The thumbnail cache in ~/.cache/thumbnails.
.TP
.B browsers
Caches of Firefox, Chrome, Chromium, Brave, Edge, Vivaldi and Opera.
Off by default; browsers that are running are skipped.
.TP
.B pip
The pip download cache in ~/.cache/pip.
.TP
.B npm
The npm cache in ~/.npm/_cacache.
.SH SAFETY
Only caches, logs, trash, unused packages and stale temporary files are
removed; documents and settings are never touched.
Deletion never follows symbolic links or crosses into other filesystems.
The privileged helper accepts only a fixed list of task names and numeric
limits, and never runs commands or paths supplied by the caller.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/sparkle-clean/settings.json
Selection and limits, shared by the app and the command line.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/sparkle-clean/history.jsonl
Record of past cleanups.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/sparkle-clean/logs/
A detailed log of each cleanup, including every file removed by the helper.
The 30 most recent logs are kept.
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/sparkle-clean/sparkle-clean-helper
The privileged helper.
.TP
.I /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/io.github.wakedog.SparkleClean.policy
Polkit policy that authorizes the helper.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or nothing to do.
.TP
.B 1
Some categories could not be cleaned (see the log).
.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments, or confirmation was needed but not possible.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show what can be cleaned:
.PP
.RS
.B sparkle-clean scan
.RE
.PP
Clean only the package cache and unused packages, without asking:
.PP
.RS
.B sparkle-clean clean \-\-only apt_cache,orphans \-\-yes
.RE
.PP
Report reclaimable space from a monitoring script:
.PP
.RS
.B sparkle-clean scan \-\-json | jq .selected_size
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR apt-get (8),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR snap (8),
.BR flatpak (1),
.BR pkexec (1)
